name Of Ash That Remembers
msg
Of Ash That Remembers
by Veyrix Scale-Eyed

It is commonly written that the Dragon Wars ended with Azuriner?s victory and the sealing of Old Scorn. This phrasing comforts historians, but it is false in a way only time can make dangerous. Wars do not end when one side rebuilds. They end when memory loosens its grip. Among dragons, memory does not loosen.

The Great Wall still stands, weathered and broken in places, yet avoided by dragonkind even now. Scholars claim this proves fear. I argue it proves restraint. Dragons remember Scorn not as a battlefield, but as a wound. They were driven from ancestral lands, then lured back through treachery, then bound, crushed, or buried beneath collapsing stone. The West calls this salvation. Dragons call it betrayal.

In the lower strata of Old Scorn, fossilized teeth remain fused into walls and streets. These are not random remains of slain beasts. Their placement follows patterns?near forums, towers, and halls of gathering?suggesting that dragons pressed into the city deliberately, not blindly. Some teeth are shattered in ways consistent with Scornian counter-weapons. Others are whole, broken only by the weight of falling buildings. This was not a rout. It was mutual ruin.

The Golden Lance of Lythander is celebrated as a slayer of dragons, yet the oldest draconic laments speak not of death, but of command. Of voices forced silent. Of wills bent skyward and away. If this is true, then Azuriner?s act was not mercy, but domination. The sealing of Old Scorn becomes less a burial of the dead, and more a prison for the truth.

The Dragon Guild insists its purpose is balance. I have seen their archives. Balance is the word chosen when vengeance is postponed, not forgiven. Dragons do not raid Scorn. They do not burn its ships. They do not return. But they watch. Their long lives grant them patience that mortals mistake for peace.

Brittany learned what happens when isolation is mistaken for safety. Scorn risks the same error. The moons still turn. Ages still pass. And dragons, unlike empires, do not forget who taught them that stone cities can fall.

Old Scorn sleeps beneath our feet, heavy with teeth, bone, and memory. Should it ever be disturbed too deeply?should relics be harvested, graves mined, or the Lance uncovered?the dragons will not need to cross the Wall again.

They will simply remind Scorn why it was built.
endmsg
