arch guide_priest
name The Devouring Of The Unshaped
msg
*Author unknown; origin predates Imperial script*

Let it be known that dragons are not born complete. They are vessels of scale and fire, awaiting refinement through essence. Thus it was taught in the elder ages that a dragon may take into itself the nature of the unshaped world?the elementals?and be remade without losing form.

The rite begins with subjugation, not slaughter. An elemental must be bound until its motion slows and its will frays. Destruction scatters essence; patience preserves it. Fire elementals are starved of fuel, earth spirits isolated from stone, air bound within sealed currents, and water drawn into stillness. Only when the elemental ceases resistance may the devouring begin.

Consumption is not of flesh, for elementals possess none. The dragon exhales its inner flame?not to burn, but to open the throat of the world. Essence is drawn inward through breath, scale, and bone simultaneously. Pain is inevitable. Failure is common.

When essence is taken correctly, change follows. Fire yields molten veins and eyes of ember. Earth thickens scale and anchors wing and claw with crushing weight. Air hollows the bones, sharpens thought, and grants mastery of unseen currents. Water cools flame, grants patience, and bends flesh toward fluid resilience. These aspects do not fade. They rewrite lineage.

The final instruction is unadorned: only dragons who remember restraint survive transformation.
endmsg
